# External artifacts the jess oracles consume, pinned by digest.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (AFD-053 S2): every jess oracle defaulted to a path under
# .scratch/, which is gitignored. Nothing in the repo pinned what was supposed to be
# there, so 'against shipped gale-nano 0.7.0' and '10/10 digests verified' were
# asserted by FILENAME AND PROSE, not mechanically — and no one could reproduce a
# single result from a clean checkout. These digests make the claim checkable.
#
# Verified 2026-09-02: the five falcon components already in .scratch/v1341 are
# BYTE-IDENTICAL to relay's published falcon-v1.134.1 assets, so this pins what was
# actually used to produce AFD-046/-051/-052, not a lookalike.
#
# format:  <dest-path-under-.scratch>  <sha256>  <locator>
#
# The locator is what tools/deps/fetch.sh uses to RECONSTRUCT the byte from a clean
# checkout. Until 2026-09-04 these said only which release the artifact came from, which
# is not enough to fetch it — the upstream NAMES differ from the local ones. Two facts
# cost real time to establish and are recorded here so nobody re-derives them:
#
#   * The falcon stages are NOT standalone release assets. They live inside
#     falcon-components-v1.134.tar.gz and are named falcon-<stage>-v1.134.wasm upstream.
#     The mapping below was established BY DIGEST, not by guessing names.
#   * gale-nano is in NO gale release (checked v0.7.0/v0.7.1/v0.8.0 — the release carries
#     only the msgq/mutex/sem primitives). It is an OCI artifact at
#     ghcr.io/pulseengine/gale-nano:0.7.0, where its name is fused-gustos.component.wasm.
#
# grammar:
#   release:<owner>/<repo>@<tag>!<asset>[!<member-inside-tarball>]
#   oci:<ref>!<file-in-artifact>
#
# PLATFORM-SPECIFIC PINS: synthpin/synth is a compiled BINARY, so its digest differs per host.
# There is therefore ONE LINE PER PLATFORM, each tagged `platform=`, and both check.sh and
# fetch.sh act only on the line matching the running host and SKIP the others visibly. A
# single-platform pin was what blocked the on-target oracles from becoming a CI gate
# (AFD-064 D4): a linux runner could never satisfy a Mach-O digest.
#
# Each linux digest below was taken from the binary inside the platform tarball AFTER
# verifying that tarball against the release's own signed SHA256SUMS.txt — so the chain is
# release signature -> tarball -> extracted binary, not a digest someone typed in.

[falcon-v1.140.0]
# BUMPED v1.139.0 -> v1.140.0. This is an INTERFACE MIGRATION, 0.10.0 -> 0.11.0 on all five
# stages, and it is the first falcon bump that carries a DISCRIMINATION HAZARD rather than
# just new bytes.
#
# THE HAZARD, measured: the v1.140 bundle ships TWO components exporting the IDENTICAL string
# `pulseengine:falcon-cascade/ekf@0.11.0` --
#     falcon-iekf  role=flight-core-stage  23,053 B  invariant EKF, Kani harnesses in CI
#     falcon-ekf   role=legacy-stage       14,750 B  LEGACY Mahony attitude filter on SO(3)
# relay's own manifest.json says of the latter: "not the estimator the falcon flight core
# uses". `wasm-tools component wit` prints the same export line for both -- so
# ifacever.find_export, which matches a version-AGNOSTIC suffix by design (AFD-121), CANNOT
# tell them apart. Pin the wrong one and every oracle downstream keeps passing, against a
# Mahony filter, silently. The pin is the last place the two are distinguishable, so the
# discriminator lives there: tools/deps/check-estimator-identity.sh, 5 controls (1 positive,
# 4 negative), wired into CI.
#
# THE MIGRATION IS A VERSION STRING, MEASURED ON ALL FIVE STAGES, not generalised from one
# (AFD-107/AFD-121): `wasm-tools component wit` output with @0.10.0/@0.11.0 normalised is
# BYTE-IDENTICAL for rate (46 lines), mixer (29), attitude (47), position (47), iekf (41) --
# 5 of 5. The comparison is not vacuous: perturbing one field type, and perturbing the
# version the normaliser rewrites, are both detected.
#
# Digests verified through the bundle's OWN signed SHA256SUMS (6/6 OK), and the iekf digest
# cross-checked against manifest.json's falcon-iekf entry -- two independent sources in the
# release agree, rather than a digest typed in from one listing.
#
# NOT BYTE-NEUTRAL IN THE LOWERED OBJECT, so every byte is attributed (the AFD-120 rule):
#   cascade.o .text 40,746 -> 40,462 (-284). Symbol set IDENTICAL, 25 distinct functions both
#   sides once the 10 aliased addresses are collapsed (each export shares an address and size
#   with a func_*, so counting both double-counts). 8 functions changed:
#     ekf#estimate/func_2    15,300 -> 15,196  -104
#     position#tick/func_13  10,774 -> 10,708   -66
#     attitude#tick/func_20   2,730 ->  2,692   -38
#     rate#tick/func_28       2,610 ->  2,582   -28
#     mixer#mix/func_25       1,188 ->  1,168   -20
#     func_3                  2,394 ->  2,374   -20
#     func_8                    686 ->    680    -6
#     func_15                   880 ->    882    +2
#   sum -280, residual -4 = alignment padding. -280 + -4 = -284, the measured delta. Nothing
#   added, nothing dropped; the five exports are RENAMED @0.10.0 -> @0.11.0, not replaced.
#
# BEHAVIOUR-NEUTRAL WHERE IT COULD BE MEASURED. 8 functions changing size is a codegen change,
# so "it lowers" is not "it works": the estimator fold over 16 ticks is BIT-IDENTICAL under
# wasmtime, 1858379312, all 14 fields at tick 1 and tick 16, IMU (0,0,-9.81,0.30,-0.15,0.07).
# The comparison is not vacuous -- perturbing gz by 1e-7 is detected. NOT COVERED locally:
# TEST-PIX-032/033 need Renode, which is not installed on this host; they run in CI.
#
# Directory stays falcon/, NOT v1140/: a version in a path is the hazard AFD-114 fixed for
# synth (fg60 -> synthpin) and it had already reappeared here once as v1341/.
falcon/rate.wasm              ea0d3a3852f2559e2407cb44689d538cb28f84a06341553a96ba5d784652f54a  release:pulseengine/relay@falcon-v1.140.0!falcon-components-v1.140.tar.gz!falcon-rate-v1.140.wasm
falcon/mixer.wasm             3416c0f288f7314ee02bddb0b4b463379a04388f5287eceb579a0cf593b3313c  release:pulseengine/relay@falcon-v1.140.0!falcon-components-v1.140.tar.gz!falcon-mixer-v1.140.wasm
falcon/attitude.wasm          ad1aea6f0ea7c05527c415f43da947f6f5ca070a543fb2b3312db9db63542096  release:pulseengine/relay@falcon-v1.140.0!falcon-components-v1.140.tar.gz!falcon-attitude-v1.140.wasm
falcon/position.wasm          55873ab0fb79ea121646f73325e9823508af7c3edba3285e572f7839f202ce9c  release:pulseengine/relay@falcon-v1.140.0!falcon-components-v1.140.tar.gz!falcon-position-v1.140.wasm
falcon/iekf.wasm              6c789664f71398937f6056736778151455aa306be62eab2285d2189607ee6163  release:pulseengine/relay@falcon-v1.140.0!falcon-components-v1.140.tar.gz!falcon-iekf-v1.140.wasm

[gale-nano]
galenano/gale-nano.wasm       6f91ded4df5939b0cb41c4d6f3444173aecaaaf40f00783bc955ec6fce271fdc  oci:ghcr.io/pulseengine/gale-nano:0.9.0!fused-gustos.component.wasm

[synth]
# NOT varve-pinned: the varve layer carries synth 0.58.0 while the campaign runs 0.77.0.
#
# BUMPED 0.64.0 -> 0.77.0 (AFD-126), thirteen releases, for a reason and not for currency: the
# first question about any lowering defect is whether a newer compiler already fixes it, and
# AFD-125 is a lowering defect. IT DOES NOT. The estimator image relinked on 0.77.0 produces all
# 31 parked words BIT-IDENTICAL to 0.64.0 — same four diverging fields, same fold 0x684578D1 —
# even though synth changed that function's codegen (ekf#estimate 15424 -> 15300 B). Reported on
# synth#1436.
#
# NOT BYTE-IDENTICAL, so every byte is attributed (the AFD-120 rule, applied to a SHRINK):
#   cortex-m7dp cascade  .text 40886 -> 40746 (-140): ekf#estimate -124, position#tick -18,
#                        rate#tick -2 = -144, plus a padding residual 26 -> 30 (+4). Identical
#                        symbol set, nothing added or dropped.
#   cortex-m3 gale-nano  .text 2340 -> 2272 (-68) across 7 functions, +2 padding. 41 symbols both.
# Behaviour bit-identical everywhere measurable: TEST-PIX-032's FROZEN baseline still exact, the
# 64-tick soak fold still 0x3466E91F, appcompose still 1068280774, all four silicon rungs build
# with REQUIRE_VERIFY=1.
#
# CARRIES A CONSEQUENCE: AFD-124 justified gale-nano 0.9.0 without hardware BECAUSE the lowered
# cortex-m3 object was byte-identical, which transferred AFD-113's on-silicon reading by
# construction. That argument held synth constant. This bump changes that object (and
# gust:os/exec#poll-round, the exact function AFD-113 is about, shrank 56 -> 44 B), so the
# reading is no longer transferred — it needs a re-run on the VLDISCOVERY, blocked on gale's host.
#
# BUMPED 0.60.0 -> 0.64.0 for synth#1189: on the ARM DIRECT SELECTOR — which is "every
# --relocatable compile", i.e. every lowering jess does — an if/else join register could
# ALIAS a live register-homed local, so `local.get` after the join read the join's value.
# Exit 0, no decline, WRONG ANSWER. Measured under 0.60.0:
#     c: mov  r0, r2      <- join writes r0, which IS local 0's home
#     e: adds r3, r0, r0  <- if-result + local.get 0, both r0
# 0.64.0 copies the home into a temp first and reads r0 intact.
#
# THE BUMP IS PROVABLY BEHAVIOUR-NEUTRAL TODAY. Every artifact jess lowers is BYTE-IDENTICAL
# across 0.60.0 -> 0.64.0: the fused cascade (m7dp, m4f), gale-nano 0.7.0 (m3), the
# f100-init and gust:hal probes (m3), and the passthrough image that runs on real F100
# silicon (which also still matches its committed .elf, md5 13495328...). And the
# differential is NOT vacuous: a module carrying the #1189 shape DOES differ (504 -> 510 B).
# So jess was never exposed, and the pin now excludes a silent wrong-answer class.
# That divergence is real and is why this is pinned by digest here (AFD-053 S5).
synthpin/synth               4b29826f1726386c72c166f536e097fcfa26d0c54e798339c866c569819f34ab  release:pulseengine/synth@v0.77.0!synth-v0.77.0-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz!synth      platform=aarch64-apple-darwin
synthpin/synth               ed3fd70a93caca4c67ee422506df8aa824bd4f25ee75f2fe3ae439343f319dad  release:pulseengine/synth@v0.77.0!synth-v0.77.0-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz!synth  platform=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu

[toolchain]
# The lowering toolchain, pinned per platform so a CI runner can reconstruct the SAME BYTES
# the varve layer dispatches locally. VERIFIED 2026-09-04: the binaries inside these release
# tarballs are byte-identical to what `varve which meld` / `varve which loom` resolve to, so
# a result measured in CI is comparable to one measured on a developer machine rather than
# merely similar.
# BUMPED 0.52.0 -> 0.55.1. jess REPORTED two of the defects its own pin still contained:
#   0.53.0  the shared-memory/rebase path could emit INVALID wasm at exit 0 (jess: meld#390)
#   0.54.0  it could emit a module that VALIDATES and returns the WRONG NUMBER (same thread)
#   0.55.0  --memory shared now validates its own output by DEFAULT — the change jess argued for
#   0.55.1  the attestation's components_fused read the FLATTENED list (jess: meld#401)
# jess fuses with --memory shared --pack-rebase, so all four are on jess's exact path.
#
# BEHAVIOUR-NEUTRAL, MEASURED END TO END. Fused CODE+DATA byte-identical 0.52.0 -> 0.55.1
# (7,949 identical lines with both metadata sections stripped), the full chain builds
# (89,816 B linked, 0 undefined, 5/5 key symbols, fusion still byte-reproducible), and the
# lowered ARM objects still match the AFD-106 baseline exactly: m7dp 5362d831..., m4f
# 90e8fa9b.... So the defect classes never fired on jess's inputs — and the pin no longer
# carries them.
#
# BUMPED AGAIN 0.55.1 -> 0.58.3 for --emit-manifest (meld#400). This bump is NOT
# byte-neutral, and the difference is fully attributed rather than waved at:
#   fused module: differs by EXACTLY ONE line, `(export "cabi_realloc" (func 2))`
#   lowered m7dp: md5 5362d831... -> d03e5912..., 41,140 -> 41,548 B (+408)
#   the +408 B is the newly-exported cabi_realloc (0x164 = 356 B) plus its callee func_1.
#   `nm` diff adds exactly {cabi_realloc, func_1} and removes nothing.
# So the AFD-106 baseline MOVES, for a stated additive reason: meld now exports the
# allocator a host needs to build guest-side arguments. Recorded, not silently adopted.
#
# What the earlier 0.52.0 -> 0.55.1 bump changed: 5 input files were attested as 10
# components under 0.52.0 and are attested as 5 under 0.55.1, with the console line agreeing.
# The attestation is the half that travels with the artifact and that an auditor reads.
# BUMPED 0.58.3 -> 0.62.0 (AFD-137), and the attribution is at the BYTE, not the symbol.
# The LOWERED OBJECT IS BYTE-IDENTICAL — cascade.o, ekf.elf and soak.elf all cmp-clean — so this
# carries zero on-target risk. The fused module grows +403 B, and every one of those bytes is
# accounted for: 31 occurrences of `,"type":"f32"` at 13 B each, added to each
# `return_area.layout` entry in the meld.signature-manifest section. Strip that literal from both
# manifests and they are BYTE-IDENTICAL; the `code` (16,862 B) and `data` (43,100 B) sections are
# unchanged. AFD-120 attributed meld's +408 B to a symbol; this attributes +403 B to a literal and
# proves the remainder identical by subtraction.
# ADOPTED FOR A REASON, not for currency: the new per-field `type` is the data whose absence caused
# AFD-127 — jess inferred `valid` as a u8 from relay's prose when the WIT declares u32, and relay
# had to correct it. tools/abi/ can now ASSERT field types instead of assuming them.
# NOTE the tarball layout changed: the binary is inside `meld-v0.62.0-<triple>/` rather than at the
# archive root. fetch.sh resolves it because it uses `find -type f -name "$member"`, which is
# recursive — worth recording, since a naive extractor would silently find nothing.
meld/meld                    d088e668be4026c1264434aa565c1921b0aa94e08c152cc7f5a102660971f516  release:pulseengine/meld@v0.62.0!meld-v0.62.0-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz!meld      platform=aarch64-apple-darwin
meld/meld                    8c05a305b904b956519088a4ab60a3cb7a20de5c2801111b41a44e8e106a7931  release:pulseengine/meld@v0.62.0!meld-v0.62.0-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz!meld  platform=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu
loom/loom                    9583f530baa0d6f0a0c140b5247ac618d9d56bcbf8d42e0c8a1ba5a2a20af6f6  release:pulseengine/loom@v1.4.0!loom-v1.4.0-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz!loom      platform=aarch64-apple-darwin
loom/loom                    5d22f8ffe1aedfb33476698978a22d93372b9214198dac33cbc6fb5df15c5380  release:pulseengine/loom@v1.4.0!loom-v1.4.0-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz!loom  platform=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu

[h1-bringup]
# NXP's i.MX RT1176 FLASHLOADER — required for H1 on real silicon.
#
# WHY IT IS NEEDED: spsdk's own device database says the RT117x ROM speaks SDP
# (usb 0x1FC9:0x013D) and that blhost/mboot (usb 0x15A2:0x0073) is only reachable AFTER a
# flashloader is running. blhost's write-memory / call / read-memory — which is exactly the
# H1 shape — therefore need this binary loaded into OCRAM first. NXP community posts claim
# RT11xx can skip it; the database disagrees, and the first read-only nxpdevscan in ISP
# settles which is true before anything is written.
#
# PROVENANCE, because this executes on the flight controller: it ships inside a TOOL repo,
# not as a release asset. Fetched from JayHeng/NXP-MCUBootUtility and verified BYTE-IDENTICAL
# to the copy in NXP's OWN org (nxp-mcuxpresso/mcu-boot-utility) — two independent sources,
# one of them the vendor. Structurally checked too: valid i.MX IVT (tag 0xD1, len 32, ver
# 0x40), entry 0x20262561 and self 0x2024FE00, both in OCRAM1 where a flashloader belongs.
# It is UNSIGNED (csf=0), which is normal for a flashloader but worth stating.
h1/ivt_flashloader.bin       ba6a8b06161aef7914436909185b6196b278f8e4cd16deeaab9945c1464d5f41  ghfile:nxp-mcuxpresso/mcu-boot-utility@master!src/targets/MIMXRT1176/ivt_flashloader.bin
# PX4's OWN command-line NSH console over MAVLink SERIAL_CONTROL. This is how `reboot -i`
# is issued without QGroundControl. Using the vendor's tool is the same category as using
# blhost or nxpdevscan — it is NOT jess building a second MAVLink stack, which is on the
# do-not-repeat list. relay owns MAVLink in this org and ships a COMMAND_LONG codec, but no
# SERIAL_CONTROL; `reboot -i` needs the console pipe, not the command interface.
#
# NOT READ-ONLY: mavlink_shell.py sends a heartbeat on connect (line 48,
# `self.mav.mav.heartbeat_send(...)`) before any command is typed. Merely attaching it is a
# WRITE to the vehicle and requires the operator's go-ahead, unlike an O_RDONLY tty capture.
#
# Pinned to the immutable commit that last touched the file, NOT @main, so the digest stays
# valid when PX4 moves.
h1/mavlink_shell.py          28cc4554d9eedf4670d1e1e200a858af4caeeab42034ed51214c3f1ab951844e  ghfile:PX4/PX4-Autopilot@de166e3a09e396a2fa1c28aa9fc014f109b25a10!Tools/mavlink_shell.py

